      "problem": "Evaluate $\\cosh  (\\ln 2).$",
      "worked_solution": "The definition of $\\cosh{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\cosh{x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}(e^{x} + e^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{-1}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+{2}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{4+1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
